# Plan one path through several poses in order, and say WHY when there is
# none.
#
# Served by planner_server (syncai_global_planner, SyncAI-Robot-Workspace) on
# the relative name `compute_path_through_poses` ->
# <robot_id>/compute_path_through_poses, IN PLACE OF
# nav2_msgs/ComputePathThroughPoses (2026-11), for the same reason as
# ComputePathToPose: the goal is nav2's, field for field, and the result adds
# error_code / error_msg. Its only client is the ComputePathThroughPoses BT
# node (syncai_behavior_tree), from the patrol tree (patrol.xml); nothing
# outside the robot calls it. For a route the failing leg matters: error_msg
# names it ("leg 3 of 5: waypoint 2 -> waypoint 3").

# The waypoints, in order, in any frame the planner server can transform into
# its global frame (map). Each pose's z picks its floor; only the last one's
# heading is kept (the path passes through the others facing the next).
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped[] goals
# Where to plan from, used only with use_start; otherwise the robot's pose.
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped start
# Which planner plugin; empty = the only one loaded (Octo3D).
string planner_id
bool use_start
---
nav_msgs/Path path
builtin_interfaces/Duration planning_time

# Why there is no path. Meaningful ONLY with status ABORTED; SUCCEEDED and
# CANCELED carry NONE, and so does a goal preempted by a newer one. A
# waypoint's reason is the goal's: GOAL_IN_KEEPOUT is any waypoint in a zone.
#
# The numbers are NavigateToGoal's and NavigateThroughGoals' 2xx, one table
# in four files (this one, ComputePathToPose, and the two navigation
# results): the navigator copies the code it gets here into its own result
# unchanged. Keep the four in step; never renumber one. Where nav2 Iron
# (ComputePathToPose) or Jazzy (ComputePathThroughPoses) has the reason, the
# number and name are theirs; 220 and up are this stack's own, for reasons
# nav2 has no code for. "Retry" says whether the same goal sent again later
# can succeed, which is what a client deciding on a retry needs to know.
uint16 NONE=0
# A failure with no finer reason: a planner that returned an empty path
# without saying why, or one that threw. Retry may help.
uint16 PLAN_FAILED=200
# planner_id names no loaded planner. Permanent.
uint16 INVALID_PLANNER=201
# The start or goal pose could not be transformed into the global frame, or
# there is no robot pose to start from. Retry may help (TF catching up).
uint16 TF_ERROR=202
# The start / the goal lies outside the map's grid. Permanent.
uint16 START_OUTSIDE_MAP=203
uint16 GOAL_OUTSIDE_MAP=204
# No traversable surface the robot can stand on near the start / the goal:
# a wall, a ledge, a floor the z hint does not pick -- or a dynamic obstacle
# (a person) covering it, which leaves. error_msg says which. Retry may help.
uint16 START_OCCUPIED=205
uint16 GOAL_OCCUPIED=206
# The search ran out of time or of iterations before reaching the goal.
# Retry may help.
uint16 TIMEOUT=207
# The search exhausted every reachable cell without reaching the goal: the
# way is closed -- by a dynamic obstacle (retry may help) or by a keepout
# zone (it will not). error_msg says which are present.
uint16 NO_VALID_PATH=208
# A route with no waypoints (ComputePathThroughPoses only). Permanent.
uint16 NO_VIAPOINTS_GIVEN=209
# No map is loaded: no octomap.bt for the map, a 2D map, or a failed load.
# Permanent until a map switch.
uint16 MAP_NOT_LOADED=220
# The map is still being built from its octomap.bt (at boot, or seconds after
# a load_map). Retry helps.
uint16 MAP_LOADING=221
# The robot stands inside a keepout zone: driven out by hand, not planned
# out. Permanent until the robot is moved.
uint16 START_IN_KEEPOUT=222
# The goal (for a route: a waypoint) lies inside a keepout zone. Permanent
# until the zone is edited.
uint16 GOAL_IN_KEEPOUT=223
# The start and the goal are on ground no stair joins (another floor with no
# stair link, an island). Permanent. A keepout zone that cuts every stair
# between them is NO_VALID_PATH: the ground is joined, the zone closes it.
uint16 DISCONNECTED=224

uint16 error_code
# Human-readable, for the task_runner log and the operator console: the
# planner's own sentence (which zone, which floor, which cells). Empty when
# error_code is NONE.
string error_msg
---
